# Rust sources and scripts are stored and checked out with LF endings.
#
# Several test files embed multi-line SQL inside ordinary Rust string literals,
# where the newline is string *content*. A CR there is a compile error ("bare CR
# not allowed in string"), so an autocrlf=true checkout of those files does not
# build. Pinning eol=lf for these types keeps the working tree identical to the
# index on every platform.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Fixtures are byte-exact inputs, not prose. Git must never rewrite them.
#
# Two failures this prevents on a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true:
#
# 1. `codex-parser18-prefix.jsonl` is compared against the decoded bytes of
#    `codex-parser18-full.jsonl.zst`, which are LF. Rewriting the plain fixture
#    to CRLF made the two disagree by exactly one byte per line (2015 vs 2004),
#    failing `pinned_compressed_rollout_matches_record_and_compaction_rows`.
# 2. The export goldens deliberately use RFC 4180 CRLF, so they must keep it;
#    but they are compared as exact bytes against generated output. Normalizing
#    either side breaks the comparison.
#
# `-text` disables all conversion and is reset below for the CRLF-by-design file,
# which declares itself binary to git while remaining CRLF on disk.
tests/fixtures/** -text
tests/fixtures/pinned/*.zst binary
